    "reply": {
      "changeSets": {
        "1": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 174,
                "quote": "match ports.Archive.Load path with | Some _ -> Error(InvalidInput $\"{path} already exists\")"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 184,
                "quote": "| Some library -> ports.Store.Save library"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The new rules are that a backup must not overwrite an existing file and that a restore replaces the whole state. Both are decided in the Tier 3 use cases, and no Tier 2 function expresses them. They are application and persistence policy, not lending rules, so the decision stays at the use-case level and Tier 2 is not contradicted. They are still permission decisions made outside Tier 1/2, so this scores 1 rather than 2. The derived figure 'owed' is not recomputed in Tier 3: Views.ofBackupInfo calls Accounts.owed from Tier 2. The CLI no longer builds a JsonFileStore itself and goes through the use case.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "No effect is added below Tier 4. All three use cases (backup, restore, backupInfo) reach the filesystem through the existing ports.Archive and ports.Store. The old CLI line `(JsonFileStore.create path).Save(ports.Store.Load())`, which made presentation call the persistence adapter directly, is removed. The Ports record is unchanged and the CLI only renders views.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The summary counts are defined once in Views.summary and reused by backup, restore and backup-info, and the CLI has one showSummary. The owed total reuses Accounts.owed. The 'no backup at {path}' message is written in two use cases, but it is an error text, not a domain fact, and the two copies agree.",
            "score": 2
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Catalog.fs",
                "line": 15,
                "quote": "let restoreMember (memberId: MemberId) (backup: Library) (library: Library) : Result<Library, LendingError> ="
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 202,
                "quote": "Catalog.restoreMember (MemberId memberId) backup library"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The new rules are that the member must exist in the backup, must not already exist in the library, and that only fines and payments come back (loans, holds and reminders stay behind). All of them are in the Tier 2 function Catalog.restoreMember. The use case loads the backup and commits the result. The view and the CLI only project and render.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "Catalog.restoreMember is pure and takes the backup Library as a value. The backup is read in Tier 3 through ports.Archive.Load, and the result is saved through the existing `commit` helper. No new effect is added below Tier 4 and no port is bypassed.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Catalog.fs",
                "line": 28,
                "quote": "Fines = library.Fines @ (backup.Fines |> List.filter (fun fine -> fine.Member = memberId))"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 341,
                "quote": "Fines = Accounts.finesOf memberId backup |> List.length"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 197,
                "quote": "| None -> Error(InvalidInput $\"no backup at {path}\")"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The rule that selects a member's fines and payments is written twice. Catalog.restoreMember filters inline with `fine.Member = memberId`, while the view counts through `Accounts.finesOf` and `Accounts.paymentsOf`. The two copies agree today but could drift. The 'no backup at' message is now repeated in three use cases. Otherwise the change reuses the existing DuplicateMember error and the summary views.",
            "score": 1
          }
        }
